{"authored" [{"question" "q-36371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(2,-3,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6(x-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-36371/review.json" "file_sha256" "32de8686459e2f013dc0476bcfcfee330623c9f4fd958c7e816b29cae598071a" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-36371/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "43e308ae67b341a21c0ae41e986afdb8d2ce7fb6f33c993d85ec0fa39dda0fd3" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-36371/verification.json" "basis" 1643 "rationale" "Correct only the isolated tangent-line prose point P(1,1,0) to P(2,-3,3), the point supplied in the unchanged prompt and consistently used in the derivative and point-slope calculation. Preserve the accepted answer, all answer fields and choices, source captures and historical versions."} {"question" "q-36371" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,-3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-3) & =6(2-1)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,-3) & ={f}_{x}(2,-3)(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6x-12 \\\\ z & =6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,-3,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-3 \\\\ z=6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6x-9$ (or $6x-z-9=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-6⟩=⟨1,0,6⟩$ is also parallel to it." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-36371/review.json" "file_sha256" "32de8686459e2f013dc0476bcfcfee330623c9f4fd958c7e816b29cae598071a" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-36371/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "43e308ae67b341a21c0ae41e986afdb8d2ce7fb6f33c993d85ec0fa39dda0fd3" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-36371/verification.json" "basis" 1643 "rationale" "Correct only the isolated tangent-line prose point P(1,1,0) to P(2,-3,3), the point supplied in the unchanged prompt and consistently used in the derivative and point-slope calculation. Preserve the accepted answer, all answer fields and choices, source captures and historical versions."} {"question" "q-36371" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨1,0,6⟩"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-36371/review.json" "file_sha256" "32de8686459e2f013dc0476bcfcfee330623c9f4fd958c7e816b29cae598071a" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-36371/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "43e308ae67b341a21c0ae41e986afdb8d2ce7fb6f33c993d85ec0fa39dda0fd3" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-36371/verification.json" "basis" 1643 "rationale" "Correct only the isolated tangent-line prose point P(1,1,0) to P(2,-3,3), the point supplied in the unchanged prompt and consistently used in the derivative and point-slope calculation. Preserve the accepted answer, all answer fields and choices, source captures and historical versions."} {"question" "q-36216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=3.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,9)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6x-y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "90aa236e45f6e067050b9e1c6a89a7a69e46a8589fec7b1586f51e8a36847f56" "basis" 1638 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-36216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,3) & =6(2)-(3)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14044096/state.json" "file_sha256" "90aa236e45f6e067050b9e1c6a89a7a69e46a8589fec7b1586f51e8a36847f56" "basis" 1638 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/mu